    "explanation": "To the rider: your account of the route is borne out and you are owed part of what you asked. To the driver: the record does not support the traffic reason you gave, and the refund follows from the policy's own matrix.\n\nWhat happened (POL-RD-22, POL-RD-21). The recommended route was PIE to the CTE, via Braddell Road and Bishan Road, 11.8 km. The trip record (EV-TRIP-05019) shows the vehicle leaving the recommended route at the PIE / Adam Road exit at 17:04:15 and rejoining at Bishan Road / Braddell Road at 17:16:09, and the driver's trace (EV-GPS-05019) places it on Adam Road and Thomson Road across that whole stretch. On the platform's recorded distances, which POL-RD-22 makes the measure, 13.1 km actual minus 11.8 km recommended gives a deviation of 1.30 km, against the 1.0 km tolerance. The deviation is established and it is over tolerance; the GPS trace is not used to re-measure it.\n\nWas the reroute traffic-justified (POL-RD-23)? No. The only traffic data in the record is the LTA advisory (EV-ADVIS-05019-01). Its type is NONE, which is not one of the closure, congestion or incident types POL-RD-23 and the policy's traffic_justification_types name, its message reads that no incident, roadwork or closure was recorded for the queried segment and window, and its justifies_reroute flag is false. The driver's statement that 'PIE was slow' is an assertion with no traffic data behind it, and POL-RD-23 says such an assertion does not justify a reroute.\n\nWhich row applies (POL-RD-24)? Not traffic-justified, deviation over 1 km: row (d). It refunds the excess distance fare in full, and that is the whole of what the policy gives on this matrix. Notification is not an element of row (d), so the driver's reliance on having 'messaged the passenger' does not change the row, and the truncated chat export (EV-CHAT-05019, IMPAIRED, weight 0.6, issue code TRUNCATED_LOG) is not needed to decide it. The impaired chat log does not carry the deciding facts here and does not lower the confidence.\n\nThe amount (POL-RD-24, POL-GEN-03). The excess distance fare is the deviation distance times fare_breakdown.per_km_rate_sgd, without surge, rounded to the cent: 1.30 km x 0.72 SGD/km = 0.94 SGD, the same figure the payments ledger's own derived money block returns (EV-FARE-05019). The rider asked for 3.00 SGD, which implies roughly 4.2 km of excess distance at that rate; the recorded deviation is 1.30 km, so the claim succeeds only to 0.94 SGD. The remaining 2.06 SGD is not supported: the base fare, time fare, platform fee and the distance fare for the kilometres actually travelled are charges for service delivered, and the formula carries no other component. The fare ledger shows payment_status CHARGED and refund_issued_sgd 0.00 (EV-FARE-05019), so nothing has yet been returned and the ruling is a PARTIAL_REFUND of 0.94 SGD. Because the rider filed, the outcome is read from the rider's claim: the rider is owed less than the 3.00 SGD contested, so this is a partial refund and the driver owes no compensation.\n\nWhy the AI decides (POL-GEN-22, POL-RD-25). The contested amount is 3.00 SGD, below POL-GEN-22's 10.00 SGD threshold, so the AI ruling is final at any confidence; both profiles carry fraud_flags 0, so no 0.95 uplift applies; and POL-RD-25's 2 km ceiling is not in force because route_deviation thresholds.ai_ruling_max_deviation_applied is false (in any event 1.30 km is under 2 km). Confidence is 0.90, above the 0.80 POL-RD-25 requires, and is supported at that level because every artifact carrying the deciding facts - the trip record, the GPS trace, the traffic advisory and the fare ledger - is complete at weight 1.0. No referral clause applies to a route deviation in this version, and no fact needed by row (d) is missing, so POL-GEN-26 is not engaged."
